  "question": {
    "text": "A small planet is revolving around a very massive star in a circular orbit of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></math> with a period of revolution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math>. If the gravitational force between the planet and the star is proportional to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math> will be proportional to",
    "image": null
  },
